ly traumatized hypothermia  is probably  the  the most uh um  the the states that they're in  um children are tired women give birth  um stressed induced labor is very common  and uh cardiac arrest as well so um but  above all they don't really know where  they are and how long it is going to  take them to to cross the border so we  try to inform them as much as possible  calm them down and take care of them by  distributing food and drinks which they  can  consume standing outside and i guess a  lot of them don't know where they're  going how how do you decide where to go  um well the first thing is that when  they arrive in poland uh the polish  border control is very well organized  and there are buses uh provided by the  firefighters which bring them to the  reception point in the shemesh which is  a town 20 kilometers away from the  border and at that point they are able  to get food they're able to shower  they're able to take a rest they're able  to sleep there um and there are private  uh private cars and uh and people are  welcoming them uh at their homes and all  over poland basically so um there's uh  there's this center which connects  drivers and hosts with refugees so so  far there hasn't been a need to set up  any refugee camp in poland even though  over a million people cross the border  and what about psychological help for  these people what what have they been  telling you about their journey across  europe's biggest country with a war  going on around them  well i had i've been escorting a couple  of families with small children from  kiev through the border  we also managed to drive a bus through  the border full of children through the  border ourselves as in volunteers  and they're they're all scared they're  all scared of helicopters of airplanes  they're scared of  of bombs they're scared of death  basically so what we're trying to do is  we're trying to comfort them i'm not a  trained psychologist but there are  people helping them in premise in poland  on the on the reception at the reception  point  michael heckman one of the volunteers  helping refugees arriving from ukraine  thank you very much for giving us uh  this insight  into the conflict thank you  now look now at some other developments  in the war police in the russian city of  saint petersburg have detained a group  of women protesting against the invasion  of ukraine they were holding up posters  which can get you arrested appalled  about the deaths of russian soldiers  moscow has passed tough penalties for  speaking out against the military  credit rating agency fitch has  downgraded russian debt by six notches  to see  saying that a default is imminent the  agency says sanctions and trade  restrictions could undermine russia's  willingness to service its debt the  western measures have thrown the  country's financial markets into turmoil  following its invasion of ukraine  the united states has dismissed an offer  by poland to send its fleet of mig-29  fighter jets to ukraine  warsaw intended to give the soviet-era  aircraft to washington for use by the  ukrainian military  the pentagon was concerned that russia  could view the three-way deal as a  hostile move  but u.s president joe biden has  announced to ban on russian oil imports  in response to the invasion it comes as  the united states struggles with rising  fuel prices and inflation the us imports  only a small percentage of its oil from  russia but the ban is expected to push  oil prices even higher  here's what president biden had to say  today i'm announcing the united states  is targeting the main artery of russia's  economy  we're banning all imports of russian oil  and gas and energy  that means russian oil will no longer be  acceptable at u.s ports and the american  people will  deal another powerful blow to putin's  war machine  this is a move that has strong  bipartisan support for congress and i  believe in the country  americans have rallied support have  rallied to 